<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android may copy off the device (D-94). Android 12 and later read this
  file; older versions read backup_rules.xml, which must say the same thing.

  ONLY the user database. Listing anything with <include> means everything not
  listed is left out, which is the point:

  - The dictionary (dictionary.db, about 100 MB) is excluded. Auto Backup is
    limited to 25 MB per app, and with the dictionary included the whole backup
    fails without telling anyone, so users' saved words were not being backed
    up at all. It is disposable anyway: it re-extracts from the APK.
  - Photos (files/scans/) are excluded. They are too large for the quota, and a
    word without a photo is a normal state, so a restore that brings words and
    lists back without their photos is correct rather than broken.
  - There are no preferences to lose; the app keeps none.

  The -wal file is included beside the database because Room writes in WAL
  mode, and recent saves can sit in it until the next checkpoint. The -shm file
  is not: it is a shared-memory index SQLite rebuilds on open.

  Device transfer (a cable or Wi-Fi move to a new phone) follows the same rule.
  It has no size limit, so photos could travel that way one day, but the
  project owner's call is that photos are never copied off the device.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="database" path="user-data.db" />
        <include domain="database" path="user-data.db-wal" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="database" path="user-data.db" />
        <include domain="database" path="user-data.db-wal" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
